        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2025-10-05T19:42:44Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I am afraid there is (currently) no way, sorry. And currently none is planned, as it would require to account for `vim.w` variables, which is too much.\r\n\r\nThere *might* be an update to have this behavior (visualize scope only in the current window) by default, but that requires research about window-scoped extmarks. Since this is Neovim>=0.11 only, it is not quite a high priority yet. Although I now can't find how to do that o.O",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I am afraid there is (currently) no way, sorry. And currently none is planned, as it would require to account for <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.w</code> variables, which is too much.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">There <em>might</em> be an update to have this behavior (visualize scope only in the current window) by default, but that requires research about window-scoped extmarks. Since this is Neovim&gt;=0.11 only, it is not quite a high priority yet. Although I now can't find how to do that o.O</p>",
            "bodyText": "I am afraid there is (currently) no way, sorry. And currently none is planned, as it would require to account for vim.w variables, which is too much.\nThere might be an update to have this behavior (visualize scope only in the current window) by default, but that requires research about window-scoped extmarks. Since this is Neovim>=0.11 only, it is not quite a high priority yet. Although I now can't find how to do that o.O",
